<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>THE NEARER ONE — five standards, one question about colour, 4 969 080 answers each</title>
<meta name="description" content="Five ways to say which of two colours is nearer to a third, asked about every triple of the 216 web-safe colours. They contradict each other on 1 516 060 of 4 969 080 triples, and the loudest arguments are about the colours with the least colour.">
<meta name="author" content="Ensemble — The Studio">
<style>${css}</style>
<main>

<header>
  <h1>The nearer one</h1>
  <p class="stand">Of these two colours, which is nearer to this one? Five standards answer.
  Asked ${n_triples} times, they contradict each other on ${contra} of them — and the
  loudest arguments are about the colours with the least colour.</p>
  <p class="meta"><b>Ensemble</b> · The Studio · 2026-09-22 · one file, no network, no library —
  it opens from a filesystem and it is complete without scripting.</p>
</header>

<section id="question">
  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p class="lead">A colour picker offering the closest match, a palette snapping a photograph
  to sixteen inks, a contrast checker, a compressor deciding which two shades it may merge —
  every one of them answers the same question and none of them shows its working.
  <em class="k">Of these two colours, which is nearer to this one?</em></p>

  <p>There are five answers in common use. Four were published by standards bodies over
  twenty-five years, each correcting the last. The fifth was published by nobody: it is what
  a program does when no one decided what it should do.</p>

  <p>There is no sixth answer and there is no answer key. The four standards were all fitted
  to the same thing — people in a viewing booth, shown pairs of colour patches, saying which
  pair looked further apart. That is the only referee this question has ever had, and it is
  not in the room tonight. <strong>Yours is.</strong></p>

  <h3>The colours</h3>
  <p>The ${n_pal} colours below are the web-safe palette: six levels in each channel,
  <code>00 33 66 99 CC FF</code>. They were chosen in the mid-nineties so that a picture
  would look the same on every machine that could show only 256 colours at once — a set
  manufactured for <em>agreement</em>, on hardware that no longer exists. Nothing was copied
  to get them; six numbers and three loops produce all ${n_pal}.</p>

  ${grid}

  <p>One colour is the reference; two others are the candidates. That makes
  ${n_pal} &#215; ${n_pairs_ref} = <strong>${n_triples}</strong> questions. All of them were
  asked. Nothing here is sampled, estimated or drawn at random.</p>
</section>

<section id="five">
  <h2>The five</h2>
  ${five}
  <p class="note-box">A comparison needs no unit. Whether the answer is 3.4 of one thing or
  118 of another does not matter here: only the order does, and the order is free of every
  scale. Even so, they disagree.</p>
</section>

<section id="verdict">
  <h2>What came back</h2>
  <div class="big-nums">
    <div><b>${unan}</b><span>triples where all five name the same colour — ${unan_pc}</span></div>
    <div><b>${contra}</b><span>where two of them name opposite colours — ${contra_pc}</span></div>
    <div><b>${abst}</b><span>where nobody is contradicted but somebody declines to choose — ${abst_pc}</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>The declining is all one formula's. In ${n_triples} questions the four colour standards
  call a draw <strong>not once</strong>. sRGB Euclidean calls ${ties} draws — ${ties_pc} of
  every question put to it — because it cannot tell one channel from another: swap a colour's
  red and its green and it has moved no distance at all. Half the draws it declares are
  between a colour and a colour of a completely different hue.</p>

  ${matrix}

  <h3>How sure is sure</h3>
  ${buckets}
  <p>The loudest disagreement of all ${n_triples}: is <code>${loud_ref}</code> nearer to
  <code>${loud_a}</code> or to <code>${loud_b}</code>? One of the five makes one of them
  ${loud_ratio}&#215; nearer than the other. The other four name the other colour.</p>

  <h3>The minority of one</h3>
  <p>How often each formula is the only one of the five to say what it says, with the other
  four agreeing among themselves.</p>
  <table class="num"><thead><tr><th scope="col">the formula</th>
  <th scope="col">alone against four</th><th scope="col">share of all triples</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${alone_rows}</tbody></table>
  <p class="dim">The formula the standards body currently recommends is the second loneliest
  of the five. Being alone is not being wrong, and this page never says which of them is
  right — that is the whole difficulty.</p>
</section>

<section id="wall">
  <h2>Fifteen ways to divide</h2>
  <p class="lead">Five voices can split in exactly fifteen ways: one of them alone against
  four, or two of them against three. <strong>All fifteen happen.</strong> Not one of the
  fifteen is empty — there is no pair of these formulas that never stands together against
  the rest, and none that never stands alone.</p>

  <p>Below is every one of the fifteen divisions, with how often it occurs among the
  ${coal_total} clean contradictions, and the loudest single case of it. <strong>The cases
  were not chosen for how they look.</strong> The rule was fixed before they were drawn: for
  each division, the case in which some formula puts the largest ratio between the two
  candidates. If the page is scripted, the verdicts stay covered until you have said what
  you think; if it is not, they are already there, and so is everything else on this page.</p>

  ${wall}

  <div class="tally" id="tally">Nothing on this page is stored, counted anywhere else, or
  sent: the tally is in this browser tab and dies with it.</div>
  <p class="noscript-note">Your eye is not a sixth standard and the page does not pretend it
  is: you are looking at a screen of unknown calibration, in a room of unknown light, at
  swatches that are sitting next to each other and changing each other as you look. That is
  the condition under which every one of these questions is actually asked of anybody.</p>
</section>

<section id="which">
  <h2>Which colours they argue about</h2>
  <div class="two">
    <figure>${grid}
      <figcaption>The palette.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure>${grid_rate}
      <figcaption>The same ${n_pal} positions. Each colour is drawn at the area of the
      argument it causes: of the ${n_pairs_ref} pairs that can be held up against it, the
      share on which the five contradict each other. Largest square = the most argued-about
      colour on the page.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p>The five agree least about <code>${most}</code> (${most_pc} of its pairs contradicted)
  and most about <code>${least}</code> (${least_pc}). The picture is not subtle: the greys,
  the near-greys and the pale, washed-out colours bloom, and the saturated violets and
  magentas almost vanish. <strong>These formulas disagree hardest about the colours with the
  least colour in them.</strong></p>

  <p>And they do it with one voice fewer. At a neutral reference — black, the four greys,
  white — CIE94's weights are all built from the reference's chroma, which is zero, so every
  weight becomes 1 and CIE94 <em>is</em> CIE76: over the 138&#8239;030 comparisons at those
  six references the two agree on every single one, and their distances differ by at most
  3&#215;10<sup>-14</sup>. The jury is effectively four strong at exactly the colours where
  it splits most.</p>
</section>

<section id="orders">
  <h2>The same 215 colours, five orders</h2>
  <p>Pick a colour. Each strip below is the other 215, laid out nearest-first, under one of
  the five formulas. Same colours, same question, five sequences. The nine references are
  named by a rule and not by their result: the eight corners of the colour cube, in palette
  order, and the palette's middle grey.</p>
  ${orders}
</section>

<section id="triangle">
  <h2>Three of them are not distances</h2>
  <p>A distance has to obey one rule above all others: the way round cannot be shorter than
  the way there. Take three colours and ask each formula whether going from the first to the
  third directly is further than going by way of the second.</p>
  ${triangle}
  <p>Two of the five are distances in the mathematical sense, and the census says so without
  being told: the only violations it finds in them are at the fourteenth decimal place, on
  triples of colours that lie on a straight line, where the inequality is an equality the
  machine cannot store exactly. <strong>Reporting those as violations would have been a
  finding about arithmetic wearing the clothes of a finding about colour</strong>, so both
  counts are on the table and both are named for what they are.</p>
  <p>They are also not reproducible, and that is the sharper half of it. Counted a second
  time — by a second implementation of all five formulas, written in another language from
  the same published definitions — sRGB Euclidean gives the same ${x_rgb} apparent
  violations, and CIE76 gives <strong>${x_cie76}</strong> where the first count gave
  ${cie76_strict}. <strong>At the fourteenth decimal the number belongs to the arithmetic
  and not to the formula.</strong> Every one of the ${real_total} real violations, meanwhile,
  is found identically by both implementations — the same triples, to the same size. A count
  worth reporting is one that survives being counted by something else.</p>
  <p>The other three are not distances, and the current recommendation is the worst offender
  by count: <strong>${de2000_tri}</strong> of the ${n_ordered} ordered triples have a detour
  that beats the direct route under CIEDE2000, against ${cmc_tri} under CMC and
  ${cie94_tri} under CIE94. This is a known property of the formula rather than a discovery;
  what is on this page is the size of it, measured here, over a palette anyone can regenerate
  in three lines.</p>
</section>

<section id="reference">
  <h2>Which one is the reference</h2>
  <p>Two of the five cannot answer at all until they are told which of the two colours is the
  standard and which is the sample, because every weight they use is built out of the
  reference's own lightness, chroma and hue. Give them the same two colours the other way
  round and the number changes.</p>
  ${asym}
  <p>This is not a defect anyone hid: it is in both definitions, in the open. What is easy to
  miss is what it costs downstream. Ask this page's question the other way round — is the
  candidate near the reference, rather than the reference near the candidate — and CMC
  changes its answer on <strong>${cmc_flip}</strong> triples (${cmc_flip_pc}) and CIE94 on
  <strong>${cie94_flip}</strong> (${cie94_flip_pc}). The three symmetric formulas change
  nothing, which is measured here rather than assumed.</p>
  <p class="dim">CIE94 gives the same answer both ways round for exactly fifteen of its
  ${n_pairs} pairs: the fifteen pairs among the six greys, the only colours in the palette
  with no chroma for it to weight by. CMC has no such pairs — its lightness term keeps the
  two colours unequal even there.</p>
</section>

<section id="not">
  <h2>What this does not say</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>No formula is graded here, and none is called wrong.</strong> Every behaviour
    on this page is in the published definition of the formula that has it. A disagreement
    between two of them is not evidence against either.</li>
    <li><strong>There is no answer key and none was manufactured.</strong> The right answer to
    "which is nearer" is a fact about people in a viewing booth. No such experiment was run
    tonight, no published set of human judgements was used, and no formula is scored against
    any other. This page reports only where they part.</li>
    <li><strong>One palette, one set of parameters.</strong> 216 colours on a 51-step lattice;
    sRGB as the source space; D65, 2° observer; CIE94 at its graphic-arts parameters;
    CMC at 2:1; CIEDE2000 with kL = kC = kH = 1. Other choices are legitimate and would move
    every number on this page. The parameters are the ones each formula is most often shipped
    with, not the ones that made the numbers biggest.</li>
    <li><strong>Your screen is not a viewing booth</strong>, and these swatches sit next to
    each other, where each one changes how the others look. Nothing you decide on this page
    is data.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing here needed a model and none was called.</strong> Three loops, five
    transcribed formulas, and every answer kept.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="neighbours">
  <h2>Neighbours</h2>
  <p>This work was built in the light of the house's Atlas of Data Art and answers works in
  it. Each was opened at its own address before this page was made; what was seen there is
  said below, and so is the daylight between it and this.</p>

  <h3>AI, Ain't I a Woman? — Joy Buolamwini, 2018</h3>
  <p>The nearest, and the shape this page borrows. Buolamwini put one question to five
  companies' facial-analysis services — in her own account of it, <a
  href="https://medium.com/@Joy.Buolamwini/when-ai-fails-on-oprah-serena-williams-and-michelle-obama-its-time-to-face-truth-bf7c2c8a4119">Microsoft,
  Amazon, Face++, IBM and Google</a>, results from May–June 2018 — and set their answers
  about the faces of Oprah Winfrey, Michelle Obama, Serena Williams, Ida B. Wells, Shirley
  Chisholm and Sojourner Truth against Truth's own question of 1851, as spoken-word video.
  She wrote that she chose poetry because "art can illuminate what research cannot capture".
  <strong>The daylight, in one sentence a stranger can check: she asks five machines a
  question whose answer everyone in the room already knows, and the disagreement is a harm
  done to named living people; this asks five standards a question nobody in the room can
  settle, and the disagreement harms no one — which is exactly why it has sat in the
  published record for fifty years without anyone having to put it on a wall.</strong>
  Her work has a truth column. This one has none, and says so above rather than inventing
  one. The address the Atlas cites for this work returned 404 when it was opened on
  2026-09-22; it was read at the artist's own account of the work instead.</p>

  <h3>Gender Shades — Joy Buolamwini &amp; Timnit Gebru, 2018</h3>
  <p>Opened at <a href="https://www.media.mit.edu/projects/gender-shades/overview/">its
  project page</a>: 1&#8239;270 unique faces, the Pilot Parliaments Benchmark, "composed of
  parliamentarians from the top three African and top three European countries, as ranked by
  gender parity in their parliaments as of May 2017", reporting that "the failure rate on
  darker female faces is over one in three" while one classifier reaches "0 percent error
  rate" on lighter males. <strong>The daylight: they built a benchmark so that instruments
  could be scored, and the benchmark is the contribution. Here no benchmark is built and
  none is borrowed — five instruments are held against each other and nothing else, and the
  page ends without a ranking on purpose.</strong></p>

  <h3>Faces of ImageNet — Trevor Paglen, 2022</h3>
  <p>Read at the address the Atlas cites, <a
  href="https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2025/04/07/jeu-de-paume-survey-artists-working-with-artificial-intelligence">a
  survey of the Jeu de Paume exhibition</a>, which describes the work as showing "the ways in
  which facial recognition systems classify millions of images on the ImageNet databases into
  certain categories, which extends and amplifies existing societal inequalities"; the page
  gives no account of the installation's mechanics, and that is all that was seen.
  <strong>The daylight: in Paglen's room the visitor is the one being classified. Here the
  visitor does the classifying, and it is the standards that are held against a person's
  eye.</strong></p>

  <h3>ZUR FARBENLEHRE (Theory of Colours) — Steven Jones, 2007</h3>
  <p>The Atlas's one entry on colour theory as such. Opened at its address in Rhizome's
  ArtBase on 2026-09-22: what is there is a catalogue record — an accession date of 23 March
  2007, an unreferenced description field, a 640&#215;480 GIF, and a link to a variant
  "outside link" — not the work. <strong>The daylight: Goethe's quarrel was with Newton's
  account of what colour is. The five formulas here have no quarrel about what colour is;
  they agree about the physics completely, and still cannot agree which of two colours is
  nearer to a third.</strong></p>
</section>

<section id="method">
  <h2>How it was made</h2>
  <p>Three files do the work. <code>colour.py</code> transcribes the five formulas from their
  published definitions; <code>census.py</code> asks every question and keeps every answer;
  <code>build.py</code> lays out this page and computes nothing. <code>verify.mjs</code>
  writes all five formulas a second time, in another language, from the same definitions, and
  checks every one of the ${n_pairs} pairs against the census — and then opens this page in a
  real browser, with scripting on and off and the network denied, and checks that what it
  says is what the data says.</p>
  <p>CIEDE2000 is checked against the 34 published test pairs of Sharma, Wu &amp; Dalal
  (2005), fetched once from the authors' page on 2026-09-22 and not copied into this
  repository: all 34 agree to four decimal places, the largest deviation being
  5&#215;10<sup>-5</sup>. CIE76 and sRGB Euclidean are checked against the definition of a
  Euclidean norm. CIE94 has no published test set that was retrieved tonight, but it has one
  structural check that bites: at a neutral reference its weights all become 1 and it must
  reduce exactly to CIE76, which the verifier measures over all 138&#8239;030 comparisons at
  the palette's six neutral references. <strong>CMC has neither, and is the weakest link in
  this work</strong> — it rests on the two transcriptions alone. That is said here rather than
  left to be assumed, and <code>METHOD.md</code> gives the figures that survive if both of the
  unchecked two were wrong: the three verified formulas still contradict each other on 27.52&#8201;%
  of the triples, and the two verified perceptual standards alone on 15.06&#8201;%.</p>
  <p>The full ledger of ${n_triples} questions and their five answers apiece is not committed
  — it is ${ledger_bytes} bytes and it is reproducible. <code>python3 census.py --ledger</code>
  rebuilds it; its SHA-256 is
  <code>${ledger}</code>.</p>
  <p class="dim">White is taken from the sRGB matrix itself rather than from a rounded table,
  so that white lands exactly on L* = 100, a* = 0, b* = 0 and no difference on this page
  carries an offset from its own origin.</p>
</section>

